Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_2.md. I didn't run the binary to exercise /cd, so I haven't seen the note appear live. The verdict rests on reading the code plus the harness's passing build and tests.

What the diff does:
• **New module:** `src/cd_config_note.rs` reads the new directory's `.yoyo.toml` and names which of `[permissions]`, `[directories]`, hooks and MCP servers it sets, saying they are NOT applied.
• **No new output for the usual cases:** a directory with no `.yoyo.toml`, or one that only sets things like `model`, prints nothing. A file that exists but can't be read gets its own message instead of being treated as empty.
• **Wiring:** `dispatch.rs` gains one line in the `/cd` success path, after the trust check and after the existing DIM line, which is kept. The file is only read; nothing from it is applied, and trust and permissions are untouched.
• **Safety details:** the path is cleaned through sanitize_for_display before printing, and plain output contains no em dash or bullets.
• **Tests:** they build their inputs from real TOML text and cover every case the task listed, plus a test for an unknown hook name and one for a hostile path.
• Docs: `docs/src/usage/commands.md and ARCHITECTURE.md` are both updated.

Where it departs from the task, both acceptable:
• There is no toml crate in Cargo.toml (I checked). So the module reuses the parsers yoyo runs at startup, and the task's "unparseable" case became the "file can't be read" case. The line-based parser never fails, so there is no separate parse-error case.
• `cd_config_note` takes a small result type rather than a list of section names, which the task explicitly allowed.

Two things I noticed but didn't fail it for:
• The existing DIM line goes to stdout and ends with a blank line; the new note goes to stderr straight after it. That's a cosmetic ordering quirk only.
• The positive control (the deliberate-breakage check) is only claimed in ARCHITECTURE.md; I didn't re-run it.
